WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.400
Less than

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.603
37 hours of breathable air estimated

00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:04.571
that's all the Coast Guard

00:00:04.571 --> 00:00:05.839
estimates has left

00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:07.374
for the five people on board

00:00:07.374 --> 00:00:08.141
a submersible

00:00:08.141 --> 00:00:09.642
that disappeared on a voyage

00:00:09.642 --> 00:00:12.078
to visit the Titanic wreckage

00:00:12.078 --> 00:00:13.747
at the bottom of the North Atlantic.

00:00:13.747 --> 00:00:15.315
A massive search and rescue effort

00:00:15.315 --> 00:00:16.783
continues some 900 miles

00:00:16.783 --> 00:00:18.952
east of Cape Cod to find that vessel

00:00:18.952 --> 00:00:20.020
which launched on Sunday.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:22.222
On board is Stockton Ross.

00:00:22.255 --> 00:00:24.157
He's the CEO of Ocean Gate.

00:00:24.157 --> 00:00:25.392
That's the expedition company

00:00:25.392 --> 00:00:27.427
that operates the submersible.

00:00:27.427 --> 00:00:28.461
Also, our

00:00:28.461 --> 00:00:30.330
with them are four other passengers.

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:30.897
CNN's Miguel

00:00:30.897 --> 00:00:32.799
Marquez has the latest

00:00:32.799 --> 00:00:33.767
from Newfoundland,

00:00:33.767 --> 00:00:35.301
where the submersible started

00:00:35.301 --> 00:00:36.503
its journey.

00:00:36.936 --> 00:00:39.172
It's this is a complex search

00:00:39.172 --> 00:00:41.074
a complex search now

00:00:41.074 --> 00:00:43.176
more complicated by time,

00:00:43.543 --> 00:00:45.779
which they're running out of.

00:00:45.779 --> 00:00:46.980
We know there's about

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:49.382
there's about 40 hours of breathable air

00:00:49.749 --> 00:00:52.552
left, deepwater submersibles and gear

00:00:52.552 --> 00:00:53.486
converging on

00:00:53.486 --> 00:00:55.355
Saint John's, Newfoundland, from the U.S.

00:00:55.355 --> 00:00:56.523
and Canada.

00:00:56.523 --> 00:00:59.292
It's the closest land to the search zone.

00:00:59.759 --> 00:01:01.461
If the Titan can be found,

00:01:01.461 --> 00:01:03.229
they'll need to bring all resources

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:05.565
to bear as quickly as possible.

00:01:05.965 --> 00:01:07.067
You're dealing with a surface

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:08.401
search and a subsurface

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:09.002
search, and frankly,

00:01:09.002 --> 00:01:09.436
that makes it

00:01:09.436 --> 00:01:11.404
an incredibly complex operation.

00:01:11.404 --> 00:01:13.073
The five person submersible

00:01:13.073 --> 00:01:14.808
started its dove around 9 a.m.

00:01:14.808 --> 00:01:16.443
Newfoundland time on Sunday,

00:01:16.443 --> 00:01:18.545
its last contact with its mothership

00:01:18.745 --> 00:01:19.913
The polar prince

00:01:19.913 --> 00:01:21.748
was an hour and 45 minutes

00:01:21.748 --> 00:01:23.583
into a dove, expected

00:01:23.583 --> 00:01:27.887
to last just over 9 hours at 6:35 p.m..

00:01:27.887 --> 00:01:29.556
Newfoundland time on Sunday.

00:01:29.556 --> 00:01:30.824
The sub was reported

00:01:30.824 --> 00:01:32.759
missing when it failed to surface

00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:35.628
at the scheduled time of 6:10 p.m..

00:01:36.096 --> 00:01:38.498
The vessel has oxygen for five people

00:01:38.498 --> 00:01:40.233
for about four days.

00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:43.403
But oxygen is only one critical element.

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:44.838
If they are alive

00:01:44.838 --> 00:01:45.438
and they're in there,

00:01:45.438 --> 00:01:46.139
they're going to be

00:01:46.139 --> 00:01:48.408
at almost freezing temperatures.

00:01:48.408 --> 00:01:50.009
Assuming they lost all their power.

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:51.377
That's why they can't communicate that

00:01:51.377 --> 00:01:53.513
it's going to be dark, cold

00:01:54.481 --> 00:01:56.749
and oxygen is there their

00:01:57.317 --> 00:01:58.351
most precious resource

00:01:58.351 --> 00:01:59.285
for so consuming that

00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:00.987
staying calm, sleeping

00:02:00.987 --> 00:02:02.689
the vessel and search area,

00:02:02.689 --> 00:02:05.058
extremely isolated and deep.

00:02:05.358 --> 00:02:07.560
Roughly 460 miles south

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:07.961
of Saint

00:02:07.961 --> 00:02:08.795
John's, Newfoundland,

00:02:08.795 --> 00:02:11.598
and 900 miles east of Boston

00:02:11.931 --> 00:02:13.967
and possibly more than two miles

00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:14.834
below the surface

00:02:14.834 --> 00:02:16.402
where pressure is nearly

00:02:16.402 --> 00:02:19.105
£6,000 per square inch.

00:02:19.572 --> 00:02:20.540
Those on board

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:22.575
adventuring British billionaire Hamish

00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:24.878
Harding, Pakistani businessman, father

00:02:24.878 --> 00:02:27.881
and son Shahzad and Suleyman Dawood.

00:02:28.281 --> 00:02:29.282
French explorer

00:02:29.282 --> 00:02:33.219
Paul Henry Nardelli and CEO and founder

00:02:33.219 --> 00:02:35.421
Stockton Rush, who owns Ocean

00:02:35.421 --> 00:02:38.057
Gate Expeditions and the missing sub

00:02:41.628 --> 00:02:42.562
so I want to give you a sense

00:02:42.562 --> 00:02:43.530
of what's happening here

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:45.064
at Saint John's Harbor right now.

00:02:45.064 --> 00:02:46.666
This is the Horizon Arctic.

00:02:46.666 --> 00:02:47.400
It's the sister

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.335
ship to the Polar Prince

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:51.171
We believe this ship may be headed out.

00:02:51.171 --> 00:02:53.339
There's a C-17 plane from the U.S.

00:02:53.339 --> 00:02:55.675
that has just landed at the airport here.

00:02:55.708 --> 00:02:57.744
It is going to bring gear down here.

00:02:58.278 --> 00:02:59.479
A coast guard ship

00:02:59.479 --> 00:03:01.147
from the Canadian Coast Guard

00:03:01.147 --> 00:03:02.749
just left the harbor here

00:03:02.749 --> 00:03:05.585
and is on its way to the search zone

00:03:05.585 --> 00:03:05.985
as well.

00:03:05.985 --> 00:03:08.087
Just a massive effort

00:03:08.087 --> 00:03:09.422
and a lot of hope

00:03:09.422 --> 00:03:12.058
that this one turns out well Jake.

00:03:12.058 --> 00:03:12.325
All right.

00:03:12.325 --> 00:03:14.194
Miguel Marquez in

00:03:14.194 --> 00:03:15.161
Saint John's, Newfoundland,

00:03:15.161 --> 00:03:15.862
thank you so much.

00:03:15.862 --> 00:03:17.330
Let's bring in Mike Reese.

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:17.597
Mr.

00:03:17.597 --> 00:03:19.432
Reese took the same voyage

00:03:19.432 --> 00:03:20.300
on an ocean gate

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:21.134
vessel to

00:03:21.134 --> 00:03:22.802
view the wreckage of the Titanic

00:03:22.802 --> 00:03:24.370
11 months ago.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:25.305
Mike, thanks for joining us.

00:03:25.305 --> 00:03:26.272
We have a photo of you

00:03:26.272 --> 00:03:28.508
on your own trip to see the wreckage.

00:03:28.508 --> 00:03:30.343
Can you tell us what your experience

00:03:30.343 --> 00:03:32.679
was like on the ocean gate voyage?

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:36.182
Yeah, it sounds a little grand

00:03:37.217 --> 00:03:38.017
in every way

00:03:38.017 --> 00:03:40.954
you felt like being a Mercury astronaut

00:03:40.987 --> 00:03:42.088
like this.

00:03:42.088 --> 00:03:43.756
This wasn't a vacation.

00:03:43.756 --> 00:03:45.091
It wasn't tourism.

00:03:45.091 --> 00:03:47.994
It was it was exploration.

00:03:47.994 --> 00:03:50.029
And you're getting on a ship.

00:03:50.029 --> 00:03:51.097
That's

00:03:51.598 --> 00:03:53.266
the best it could be.

00:03:53.266 --> 00:03:55.501
But they're learning as they go along.

00:03:55.501 --> 00:03:56.236
And you

00:03:56.236 --> 00:03:58.471
you get on with a lot of excitement,

00:03:59.138 --> 00:04:01.307
but just constant trepidation,

00:04:01.307 --> 00:04:04.644
constantly knowing this could be the end.

00:04:04.677 --> 00:04:05.745
And in fact,

00:04:05.745 --> 00:04:07.747
I was supposed to go with my wife

00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:08.748
and she tested

00:04:08.748 --> 00:04:10.049
positive for COVID

00:04:10.049 --> 00:04:12.418
right before we got on.

00:04:12.418 --> 00:04:15.822
And so I kissed her goodbye, knowing

00:04:16.689 --> 00:04:17.790
that that might be

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:19.626
the last time I'd ever seen her.

00:04:19.626 --> 00:04:21.761
So nobody walked into this,

00:04:22.395 --> 00:04:23.162
you know, thinking

00:04:23.162 --> 00:04:25.231
it was going to be a pleasure cruise.

00:04:25.231 --> 00:04:29.002
And I, especially the the experts on bald

00:04:29.369 --> 00:04:31.037
fact and Rose,

00:04:31.037 --> 00:04:32.005
you know,

00:04:32.005 --> 00:04:33.439
they made it as safe

00:04:33.439 --> 00:04:34.941
as they could make it.

00:04:34.941 --> 00:04:37.010
They trusted their own lives to

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:37.877
it, but they knew

00:04:38.945 --> 00:04:40.947
it could end this way.

00:04:40.947 --> 00:04:41.581
Mm hmm.

00:04:41.781 --> 00:04:43.416
You did a podcast about your trip,

00:04:43.416 --> 00:04:44.517
and you described

00:04:44.517 --> 00:04:46.653
how the ship is controlled by a joystick

00:04:46.653 --> 00:04:48.187
from a gaming console,

00:04:48.187 --> 00:04:50.657
much like an Xbox controller.

00:04:50.657 --> 00:04:52.825
Tell us about the technology

00:04:52.825 --> 00:04:54.627
and how it works.

00:04:55.228 --> 00:04:55.962
Yes.

00:04:56.162 --> 00:04:58.798
As a lot of people have had focus on

00:04:58.798 --> 00:04:59.866
that as it

00:04:59.866 --> 00:05:02.101
this is some some jerry-rigged,

00:05:02.935 --> 00:05:05.204
you know, crazy little jalopy

00:05:05.204 --> 00:05:06.372
that's been slapped together

00:05:06.372 --> 00:05:08.641
but it's one of the most reassuring

00:05:08.641 --> 00:05:10.576
things about the submarine

00:05:10.576 --> 00:05:12.712
is how very simple.

00:05:12.712 --> 00:05:16.649
And once it's a pressure resistant to it

00:05:16.649 --> 00:05:17.850
basically drops

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:19.385
to the bottom of the ocean.

00:05:19.385 --> 00:05:20.086
It took

00:05:20.086 --> 00:05:21.587
two and a half hours to go

00:05:21.587 --> 00:05:22.989
kind of straight down.

00:05:22.989 --> 00:05:26.059
And I actually fell asleep on the on

00:05:26.092 --> 00:05:27.026
the trip down.

00:05:27.026 --> 00:05:27.226
That's

00:05:27.226 --> 00:05:29.095
how kind of relaxing

00:05:29.095 --> 00:05:31.564
and meditative the whole thing is.

00:05:31.564 --> 00:05:33.533
And once you reach the ground,

00:05:33.533 --> 00:05:36.402
the ship is sort of piloted by two things

00:05:36.402 --> 00:05:38.338
that just look like a fan

00:05:38.338 --> 00:05:40.773
you would have on your desk. Very simple.

00:05:41.207 --> 00:05:41.708
And it

00:05:41.708 --> 00:05:43.743
is controlled by a joystick

00:05:43.743 --> 00:05:45.611
from a gaming console.

00:05:45.611 --> 00:05:48.581
So that even I was able to steer

00:05:48.581 --> 00:05:51.250
and navigate the submarine for a while.

00:05:51.651 --> 00:05:53.686
And again, these are all to the good.

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:55.455
I mean, simplicity

00:05:55.455 --> 00:05:55.955
I think

00:05:55.955 --> 00:05:57.123
would be

00:05:57.123 --> 00:05:59.359
having something overly complicated

00:05:59.359 --> 00:05:59.792
where you're

00:06:00.827 --> 00:06:03.196
at the mercy of a lot of technology.

00:06:03.196 --> 00:06:05.331
Nobody understands.

00:06:05.331 --> 00:06:06.032
Yeah.

00:06:06.866 --> 00:06:09.135
You also talked on your podcast

00:06:09.135 --> 00:06:10.970
about how you had to sign a waiver

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:11.504
that warned

00:06:11.504 --> 00:06:13.973
you at least twice that you could die.

00:06:14.574 --> 00:06:15.808
I want to show this little clip.

00:06:15.808 --> 00:06:16.609
CBS News

00:06:16.609 --> 00:06:19.679
reported on this Ocean Gate submersible

00:06:20.613 --> 00:06:23.316
and described another time when the ship

00:06:23.716 --> 00:06:25.852
got lost Let's take a listen

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:29.489
There's no G.P.S.

00:06:29.489 --> 00:06:30.690
underwater.

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:32.792
So the surface ship is supposed to guide

00:06:32.792 --> 00:06:33.326
the sub

00:06:33.326 --> 00:06:35.928
to the shipwreck by sending text messages

00:06:36.829 --> 00:06:38.097
turn 30 degrees, right?

00:06:38.097 --> 00:06:39.999
Probably 30 degrees.

00:06:39.999 --> 00:06:41.734
But on this dove, communications

00:06:41.734 --> 00:06:43.202
somehow broke down.

00:06:43.202 --> 00:06:45.738
The sub never found the wreck.

00:06:45.738 --> 00:06:46.973
We were lost.

00:06:46.973 --> 00:06:49.475
We were lost for two and a half hours.

00:06:50.309 --> 00:06:52.211
So this this appears to have happened

00:06:52.211 --> 00:06:52.612
before

00:06:52.612 --> 00:06:53.479
at least

00:06:53.479 --> 00:06:54.814
getting getting lost

00:06:54.814 --> 00:06:56.215
and losing communications.

00:06:57.750 --> 00:07:00.420
I have to say, I took

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:02.688
four different dives with the company,

00:07:02.688 --> 00:07:05.525
one of the Titanic and three of

00:07:05.625 --> 00:07:07.527
of New York City

00:07:07.527 --> 00:07:09.729
and communication with lost,

00:07:10.263 --> 00:07:13.499
at least briefly every single time.

00:07:13.533 --> 00:07:16.769
It just seems baked into the system.

00:07:16.769 --> 00:07:17.403
I don't know.

00:07:17.403 --> 00:07:18.971
I don't blame the submarine.

00:07:18.971 --> 00:07:21.841
As much as I blame deep water.

00:07:21.841 --> 00:07:23.810
But you would always lose it

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:26.746
and come back. And one thing is just the

00:07:26.746 --> 00:07:28.714
the safety involved

00:07:28.714 --> 00:07:31.050
these kind of things where

00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:32.885
there was one dove we took

00:07:32.885 --> 00:07:35.254
as soon as communication went up,

00:07:35.254 --> 00:07:37.123
we went right back to the surface.

00:07:37.123 --> 00:07:39.459
We were we'd go undersea

00:07:39.459 --> 00:07:42.495
U-boat just off the shore of New York.

00:07:42.962 --> 00:07:44.030
And we saw

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:45.498
we saw it for one second

00:07:45.498 --> 00:07:47.099
and they said, we're going back up.

